{"person_id":"fe-011-01-sherri-nichols","name":"Sherri Nichols","cohort_group":"Researchers / independent engineers","category":"Computer scientist / baseball statistician","birth_year":1962,"age_at_milestone":23,"milestone_by_age_26":"Co-created the Andrew File System at Carnegie Mellon, co-authoring foundational papers at ACM SOSP 1985 and 1987 before age 26, work later recognized with the 2016 ACM Software System Award.","early_history_summary":"Nichols grew up in Clarksville, Tennessee, excelling in math and bonding with her father over baseball. She earned a physics degree at Tennessee Tech before attending Carnegie Mellon for graduate computer science, where she joined the Andrew Project at the Information Technology Center and contributed to the pioneering Andrew File System.","primary_leverage_engine":"Technical depth","normalized_primary_engine":"Scarce technical / intellectual depth","secondary_engine":"Elite institutional ecosystem","started_serious_reps_before_20_score":0,"prior_reps_score":1,"scarce_skill_depth_score":2,"native_distribution_score":0,"elite_ecosystem_network_score":2,"complementary_team_score":1,"structural_wave_score":2,"concentration_intensity_score":1,"capital_safety_score":0,"domain_proximity_score":1,"total_leverage_score":10,"success_tier":3,"leverage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","family_context_summary":"Grew up in Clarksville, Tennessee; father bonded with her over baseball fandom for the Cincinnati Reds. No evidence of exceptional financial or educational advantage in reviewed sources.","parent_family_domain_summary":"Not documented in reviewed sources.","early_family_financial_platform_support_score":0,"parent_family_domain_advantage_score":0,"inherited_audience_business_network_score":0,"elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score":2,"frontier_geography_ecosystem_score":1,"rare_early_tools_facilities_score":1,"dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score":1,"exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score":1,"early_online_platform_community_score":1,"direct_customer_domain_exposure_score":0,"prodigy_physical_edge_score":0,"adversity_constraint_catalyst_score":1,"inherited_access_stack_count":7,"exceptional_inherited_access_count":1,"all_documented_early_condition_count":7,"primary_early_advantage_archetype":"Institutional ecosystem acceleration","primary_early_advantage_tags":"Carnegie Mellon, IBM partnership, distributed systems, Usenet community, gender barrier","evidence_summary":"Nichols leveraged her position as a graduate student at Carnegie Mellon's Information Technology Center, a joint CMU-IBM initiative, to co-create the Andrew File System, a pioneering distributed file system. She co-authored foundational papers at ACM SOSP in 1985 and 1987, both before age 26, and later received the 2016 ACM Software System Award for this work. She also contributed to early sabermetrics via Usenet's rec.sport.baseball community, creating Nichols' Law of Catcher Defense and co-creating Defensive Average. As a woman in both computer science and baseball analytics, she faced structural barriers but used them as motivation.","early_advantage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","source_count":4,"primary_source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherri_Nichols","source_urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherri_Nichols","https://www.theringer.com/2018/02/20/mlb/sherri-nichols-baseball-sabermetric-movement","https://www.cs.cmu.edu/news/2017/acm-honors-satya-colleagues-andrew-file-system","https://awards.acm.org/binaries/content/assets/press-releases/2017/may/technical-awards-2016a.pdf"],"annotation_status":"subagent_researched_beta","source_audit_status":"not_independently_audited","data_version":"0.3.0-trajectory-beta","eligibility_status":"age_26_eligible","starting_point":"Born in Clarksville, Tennessee to a family that bonded over baseball; father was a Cincinnati Reds fan. No evidence of exceptional financial or educational advantage.","current_position":"Serves on public boards in Redmond, Washington, including a truancy panel and planning commission; received 2016 ACM Software System Award for co-creating the Andrew File System.","current_position_year":2025,"is_living":true,"trajectory":[{"year":1984,"age":22,"event":"Graduated from Tennessee Tech with a degree in physics before beginning graduate studies in computer science at Carnegie Mellon."},{"year":1985,"age":23,"event":"Co-authored 'The ITC Distributed File System: Principles and Design' at ACM SOSP, a foundational paper for the Andrew File System."},{"year":1987,"age":25,"event":"Co-authored 'Scale and Performance in a Distributed File System' at ACM SOSP, further advancing AFS research."},{"year":1989,"age":27,"event":"Consulted briefly for the Pittsburgh Pirates on a statistical project, her lone brush with a major league team."},{"year":1990,"age":28,"event":"Pioneered early sabermetrics work including Nichols' Law of Catcher Defense and co-creation of Defensive Average via Usenet's rec.sport.baseball community."},{"year":2000,"age":38,"event":"Stepped away from baseball analytics and transitioned to community service in Redmond, Washington."},{"year":2016,"age":54,"event":"Received the ACM Software System Award for co-creating the Andrew File System, recognized as the first distributed file system designed for tens of thousands of machines."}]}
